{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Oregon's pioneering effort to decriminalize drug possession concludes this Sunday, marking a shift as new legislation reinstates criminal penalties for holding small quantities of hard substances. This measure, known as HB4002, offers individuals found with illicit items like fentanyl, heroin, and meth a choice between facing criminal charges or entering a treatment pathway. While the goal is to facilitate law enforcement actions in public spaces, the policy also introduces stricter punishments for selling drugs near parks.\n\n***\n\nUnder the new rules, simple possession becomes a misdemeanor carrying a potential six-month jail sentence. Although the law suggests counties establish diversion programs to steer people toward mental health and addiction services, it does not force them to do so. Supporters argue this flexibility allows local governments to tailor solutions to their specific resources, whereas critics fear it will result in an inconsistent and unfair system across different regions.\n\n***\n\nPortland Police Chief Bob Day highlighted the strict criteria required for deflection, noting that applicants must have no other charges, warrants, or history of violence, and must be medically stable. If an individual qualifies, officers contact a dispatch line to arrange a meeting with a mobile health team. The team has exactly thirty minutes to arrive; if they fail to appear within that window, the person is taken to jail, as officers cannot wait indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nThis legislative change reflects a broader debate on how the **country** approaches addiction, a topic that affects every **age** group. While some argue that policies should **eat** into the resources of incarceration, others believe in a balanced approach that does not disrupt daily **shopping** or public safety. As the law takes effect, the metaphorical **tongue** of the state seems poised to speak louder on enforcement than on pure compassion.\n\nP.P.S This summary captures the essence of the transition from decriminalization to a new hybrid model of enforcement and treatment.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -7.152583e-06}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.04453812}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.18585633}, {"token": " effort", "logprob": -3.098658}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -1.573575e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.007028966}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.056775115}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.0023176088}, {"token": " concludes", "logprob": -3.3020382}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.014966226}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -0.011905449}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.110933445}, {"token": " marking", "logprob": -0.905704}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0023054811}, {"token": " shift", "logprob": -0.34657875}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -2.442151}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -1.0436975}, {"token": " legislation", "logprob": -0.003435187}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -0.21294963}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -0.0039514787}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.008088189}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " holding", "logprob": -0.0009251343}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -2.6226387e-05}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.09361162}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -0.005702152}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.00241607}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.002757533}, {"token": " This", "logprob": -0.30180988}, {"token": " measure", "logprob": -3.6671205}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.04832295}, {"token": " known", "logprob": -0.027833756}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}